1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,835
This is ECE 358, video 9. The topic is Selective Repeat and the window size of pipelined protocols.

2
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:15,917
After this video, you can explain the sender and receiver windows of Selective Repeat.

3
00:00:16,367 --> 00:00:19,759
You can trace Selective Repeat when a packet is lost.

4
00:00:20,209 --> 00:00:24,369
You can find the largest window size for a sequence number space.

5
00:00:24,819 --> 00:00:29,683
You can calculate the sender utilization and the ideal window size.

6
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:36,831
At the end, we solve QB questions 30 to 35, and Problem 3 of Problem Set 3.

7
00:00:37,281 --> 00:00:50,401
First, the problem that Selective Repeat solves. This is Go-Back-N from video 8. The window holds 4 sequence numbers, 0 to 3. So the sender sends packets 0 to 3.

8
00:00:50,851 --> 00:00:53,113
Packet 2 is lost on the way.

9
00:00:53,563 --> 00:00:59,920
Packets 0 and 1 arrive. The receiver delivers them, and sends ack 0 and ack 1.

10
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:05,959
Each ACK moves the sender window by one. So the sender sends packets 4 and 5.

11
00:01:06,409 --> 00:01:15,049
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive out of order. The Go-Back-N receiver discards them. Each time, it sends ack 1 again.

12
00:01:15,499 --> 00:01:18,017
The sender ignores these duplicate ACKs.

13
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:21,027
Then the timer of packet 2 expires.

14
00:01:21,477 --> 00:01:29,135
The sender sends packets 2, 3, 4 and 5 again. The receiver now gets them in order and delivers them.

15
00:01:29,585 --> 00:01:36,071
So one lost packet costs 4 transmissions. With a large window, this wastes much of the link.

16
00:01:36,521 --> 00:01:42,366
Selective Repeat changes both ends. Again the sender sends packets 0 to 5.

17
00:01:42,816 --> 00:01:44,437
Packet 2 is lost.

18
00:01:44,887 --> 00:01:50,690
Packets 0 and 1 arrive in order. The receiver delivers them, and ACKs each one.

19
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:59,545
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive after a gap. The Selective Repeat receiver does not discard them. It buffers them.

20
00:01:59,995 --> 00:02:05,051
It ACKs each of them individually: ack 3, ack 4 and ack 5.

21
00:02:05,501 --> 00:02:10,792
A packet is correctly received when its sequence number is in the receive window.

22
00:02:11,242 --> 00:02:13,930
The sender keeps one timer for each packet.

23
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:20,012
Only the timer of packet 2 expires. So the sender resends only packet 2.

24
00:02:20,462 --> 00:02:23,086
Packet 2 fills the gap at the receiver.

25
00:02:23,536 --> 00:02:31,771
Now the receiver delivers 2, 3, 4 and 5 in order to the upper layer. One loss costs one retransmission.

26
00:02:32,221 --> 00:02:40,946
Here is the Selective Repeat sender window of slide 39. Each box is one sequence number, from 0 to 23.

27
00:02:41,396 --> 00:02:45,001
The colour of a box gives the state of its sequence number.

28
00:02:45,451 --> 00:02:51,126
The window has N equal to 12 consecutive sequence numbers, from 7 to 18.

29
00:02:51,576 --> 00:02:56,952
send base points to the oldest packet that is not ACKed. Here it is 7.

30
00:02:57,402 --> 00:03:09,242
The green boxes, 9, 10, 12 and 13, are already ACKed. Their ACKs arrived out of order. The Selective Repeat sender records each ACK on its own.

31
00:03:09,692 --> 00:03:16,007
The orange boxes, 7, 8, 11 and 14, are sent, but not yet ACKed.

32
00:03:16,457 --> 00:03:19,593
Each of these orange packets has its own timer.

33
00:03:20,043 --> 00:03:24,672
The blue boxes, 15 to 18, are in the window, but not yet used.

34
00:03:25,122 --> 00:03:30,263
next seq num points to 15. It is the smallest unused sequence number.

35
00:03:30,713 --> 00:03:37,156
The white boxes outside the window, 0 to 6 and 19 to 23, are not usable.

36
00:03:37,606 --> 00:03:44,817
Now ACKs arrive. This is the start state again. send base is 7, and the window is 7 to 18.

37
00:03:45,267 --> 00:03:49,363
ACK 7 arrives. The sender marks 7 as ACKed.

38
00:03:49,813 --> 00:03:59,818
7 is equal to send base. So send base moves to the next unACKed sequence number, 8. The window becomes 8 to 19.

39
00:04:00,268 --> 00:04:03,895
Then ACK 8 arrives. 8 is equal to send base.

40
00:04:04,345 --> 00:04:11,065
The next numbers, 9 and 10, are already ACKed. So send base does not stop at them.

41
00:04:11,515 --> 00:04:19,920
send base jumps to 11, the next unACKed number. The window becomes 11 to 22, as on the slide.

42
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:24,231
Now the 8 boxes from 15 to 22 are usable.

43
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:34,196
Of these, 19 to 22 are new in the window. The window moves only when the oldest unACKed packet is ACKed.

44
00:04:34,646 --> 00:04:40,897
Slide 40 starts from the same state. send base is 7, and next seq num is 15.

45
00:04:41,347 --> 00:04:43,779
The timer of packet 7 expires.

46
00:04:44,229 --> 00:04:49,519
The sender resends packet 7 only, and restarts the timer of packet 7.

47
00:04:49,969 --> 00:04:58,759
Packets 8, 11 and 14 are also not ACKed. But their timers did not expire, so they are not sent again.

48
00:04:59,209 --> 00:05:01,897
Then 2 new packets come from the upper layer.

49
00:05:02,347 --> 00:05:09,045
The next sequence number, 15, is in the window. So the sender sends them as 15 and 16.

50
00:05:09,495 --> 00:05:15,767
Now next seq num is 17. The drawing on slide 40 keeps the pointer at 15.

51
00:05:16,217 --> 00:05:22,511
A timeout does not move the window. The sender only resends the one packet that timed out.

52
00:05:22,961 --> 00:05:31,131
Slide 46 lists the rules of the sender for three events. The examples use a window of N equal to 4.

53
00:05:31,581 --> 00:05:34,568
First event: data comes from the upper layer.

54
00:05:35,018 --> 00:05:38,453
The next sequence number is 2. It is in the window.

55
00:05:38,903 --> 00:05:43,916
So the sender sends packet 2, and starts its timer. Box 2 turns orange.

56
00:05:44,366 --> 00:05:48,889
Second event: the timer of packet n expires. Here n is 1.

57
00:05:49,339 --> 00:05:55,632
The sender resends packet 1 only, and restarts its timer. The window does not change.

58
00:05:56,082 --> 00:05:59,986
Third event: ACK n arrives. Here ACK 0 arrives.

59
00:06:00,436 --> 00:06:08,564
The sender marks 0 as ACKed. 0 is equal to send base, and packet 1 is already ACKed.

60
00:06:09,014 --> 00:06:16,225
So send base moves to 2, the next unACKed sequence number. The window becomes 2 to 5.

61
00:06:16,675 --> 00:06:20,344
The sender uses ACK n only if n is in its window.

62
00:06:20,794 --> 00:06:31,909
The receiver also keeps a window. Here packets 8, 9 and 11 arrived out of order. The receiver buffered them and ACKed each one. They are green.

63
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:39,740
Packets 7 and 10 are expected, but not yet received. They are orange. They block the in-order delivery.

64
00:06:40,190 --> 00:06:46,995
receive base is the smallest sequence number that is expected and not yet received. Here it is 7.

65
00:06:47,445 --> 00:06:55,979
The window starts at receive base and has N equal to 12 numbers, 7 to 18. The blue numbers in it are acceptable.

66
00:06:56,429 --> 00:07:00,503
Now packet 7 arrives. It is equal to receive base.

67
00:07:00,953 --> 00:07:05,348
The receiver delivers 7, and then the buffered 8 and 9, in order.

68
00:07:05,798 --> 00:07:12,795
Packet 10 is still missing. So receive base moves to 10, and the window becomes 10 to 21.

69
00:07:13,245 --> 00:07:23,357
If packet 10 arrives next, the receiver delivers 10 and the buffered 11. Then receive base is 12, and the window is 12 to 23.

70
00:07:23,807 --> 00:07:33,429
Slide 46 also lists the rules of the receiver. Packet n arrives. In this example, receive base is 10, and N is 6.

71
00:07:33,879 --> 00:07:39,063
First case: n is in the window, 10 to 15. The receiver sends ACK n.

72
00:07:39,513 --> 00:07:47,129
It buffers an out-of-order packet. It delivers an in-order packet with the buffered packets after it, and moves the window.

73
00:07:47,579 --> 00:07:54,576
Second case: n is in the previous window, 4 to 9. The receiver already delivered this packet.

74
00:07:55,026 --> 00:07:57,266
So it only sends ACK n again.

75
00:07:57,716 --> 00:08:02,303
Third case: any other sequence number. The receiver ignores the packet.

76
00:08:02,753 --> 00:08:10,177
Why is the second case necessary? Here packet 9 arrives. The receiver delivers it and sends ack 9.

77
00:08:10,627 --> 00:08:12,376
But ack 9 is lost.

78
00:08:12,826 --> 00:08:17,626
The timer of packet 9 expires, and the sender resends packet 9.

79
00:08:18,076 --> 00:08:25,543
9 is now in the previous window. The receiver does not deliver it again, but it sends ack 9 again.

80
00:08:25,993 --> 00:08:29,982
Without this ACK, the sender window never moves past 9.

81
00:08:30,432 --> 00:08:39,776
Here is Selective Repeat in action, with the same loss as before. The window is again 4. The sender sends packets 0 to 3.

82
00:08:40,226 --> 00:08:41,847
Packet 2 is lost.

83
00:08:42,297 --> 00:08:48,655
Packets 0 and 1 arrive. The receiver delivers them, and sends ack 0 and ack 1.

84
00:08:49,105 --> 00:08:53,478
The sender window moves, and the sender sends packets 4 and 5.

85
00:08:53,928 --> 00:09:02,440
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive out of order. The receiver buffers each one, and sends ack 3, ack 4 and ack 5.

86
00:09:02,890 --> 00:09:10,122
The sender records each of these ACKs. send base stays at 2, because packet 2 is not ACKed.

87
00:09:10,572 --> 00:09:12,855
The timer of packet 2 expires.

88
00:09:13,305 --> 00:09:18,510
The sender resends packet 2 only. It does not resend 3, 4 or 5.

89
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:26,491
Packet 2 arrives. The receiver delivers packets 2, 3, 4 and 5 in order, and sends ack 2.

90
00:09:26,941 --> 00:09:33,319
The slide asks: what happens when ack 2 arrives? Ack 2 ACKs the send base, packet 2.

91
00:09:33,769 --> 00:09:42,281
Packets 3, 4 and 5 are already ACKed. So send base moves to 6, and the window becomes 6 to 9.

92
00:09:42,731 --> 00:09:45,462
One loss cost only one retransmission.

93
00:09:45,912 --> 00:09:52,376
Before the dilemma, we need one fact. The sequence number is a field of k bits in the packet header.

94
00:09:52,826 --> 00:10:01,829
So it has 2 to the power k values, from 0 to 2 to the power k, minus 1. This set is the sequence number space.

95
00:10:02,279 --> 00:10:09,468
For example, k equal to 2 gives 2 to the power 2, which is 4 values: 0, 1, 2 and 3.

96
00:10:09,918 --> 00:10:14,014
Packets 0 to 3 get the sequence numbers 0 to 3.

97
00:10:14,464 --> 00:10:23,125
After 3, the numbers start again at 0. Packet 4 gets sequence number 0, packet 5 gets 1, and so on.

98
00:10:23,575 --> 00:10:27,885
So packets 0, 4 and 8 all carry sequence number 0.

99
00:10:28,335 --> 00:10:33,561
The receiver sees only the number. It cannot tell which of these packets arrived.

100
00:10:34,011 --> 00:10:41,798
Slide 48 shows a dilemma. The sequence numbers are 0, 1, 2 and 3. The window size is 3.

101
00:10:42,248 --> 00:10:45,021
The sender sends packets 0, 1 and 2.

102
00:10:45,471 --> 00:10:52,511
After each packet, the receiver window moves by one. Now the receiver accepts 3, 0 and 1.

103
00:10:52,961 --> 00:11:01,025
In case a, ACK 0 and ACK 1 arrive. The sender window moves to 1, 2, 3, and then to 2, 3, 0.

104
00:11:01,475 --> 00:11:08,046
The sender sends packet 3, which is lost. Then it sends a new packet with sequence number 0.

105
00:11:08,496 --> 00:11:15,643
The receiver accepts sequence number 0. It is the new packet 0. This is correct. No problem.

106
00:11:16,093 --> 00:11:20,850
Case b uses the same sequence numbers and the same window size.

107
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:24,073
The sender sends packets 0, 1 and 2.

108
00:11:24,523 --> 00:11:28,833
The receiver accepts them, and its window becomes 3, 0, 1.

109
00:11:29,283 --> 00:11:31,608
But now all three ACKs are lost.

110
00:11:32,058 --> 00:11:37,903
The timer of packet 0 expires. The sender retransmits the old packet 0.

111
00:11:38,353 --> 00:11:46,268
The receiver window, 3, 0, 1, contains 0. So the receiver accepts this old copy as new data.

112
00:11:46,718 --> 00:11:51,433
So the data of packet 0 is delivered twice. This is a problem.

113
00:11:51,883 --> 00:12:01,205
In both cases, the receiver window is 3, 0, 1, and the receiver sees sequence number 0. It cannot know which case it is in.

114
00:12:01,655 --> 00:12:10,189
Why does this happen? Take a window of 3. The sender can still resend its oldest 3 sequence numbers: 0, 1 and 2.

115
00:12:10,639 --> 00:12:17,167
If all ACKs are lost, the receiver already accepts the next 3 numbers: 3, 0 and 1.

116
00:12:17,617 --> 00:12:22,246
That is 3 plus 3, which is 6 numbers, in a space of only 4.

117
00:12:22,696 --> 00:12:31,016
So the numbers 0 and 1 are in both groups. A packet with sequence number 0 can be an old copy or new data.

118
00:12:31,466 --> 00:12:38,634
With a window of 2, the two groups use 2 plus 2, which is 4 numbers. That fits in the space of 4.

119
00:12:39,084 --> 00:12:46,551
Each number has one meaning. The receiver accepts only 2 and 3. So an old packet 0 is not accepted.

120
00:12:47,001 --> 00:12:52,547
So the window size must be less than or equal to half of the sequence number space.

121
00:12:52,997 --> 00:12:58,715
The slide's answer: reduce the window size to 2, which is 4 divided by 2.

122
00:12:59,165 --> 00:13:07,165
Now the performance of a sliding window when there are no errors. The sender sends the first bit of a packet at time 0.

123
00:13:07,615 --> 00:13:13,183
The last bit leaves at time L over R. L over R is the transmission time.

124
00:13:13,633 --> 00:13:20,865
With a window of W packets, the sender sends W packets back to back. Here W is 3.

125
00:13:21,315 --> 00:13:25,133
So the sender is busy for W times L over R.

126
00:13:25,583 --> 00:13:30,938
The first bit arrives at the receiver one propagation delay later, at d prop.

127
00:13:31,388 --> 00:13:38,364
When the last bit of a packet arrives, the receiver sends an ACK. Here the ACK has no transmission time.

128
00:13:38,814 --> 00:13:46,323
The ACK needs one more propagation delay. So it arrives at the sender at L over R, plus 2 d prop.

129
00:13:46,773 --> 00:13:54,944
2 d prop is the round-trip time, RTT. So the first ACK arrives at RTT plus L over R.

130
00:13:55,394 --> 00:13:58,914
Before that, the window is full, and the sender is idle.

131
00:13:59,364 --> 00:14:04,249
The time from 0 to RTT plus L over R is one cycle.

132
00:14:04,699 --> 00:14:11,526
When the ACK arrives, the window moves, and the sender sends the next packet. The same cycle repeats.

133
00:14:11,976 --> 00:14:17,800
Now the sender utilization. U sender is the fraction of time that the sender sends.

134
00:14:18,250 --> 00:14:27,359
This example uses L over R equal to 1 ms, and an RTT of 9 ms. These are our own numbers.

135
00:14:27,809 --> 00:14:35,447
One cycle lasts RTT plus L over R. Here that is 9 plus 1, which is 10 ms.

136
00:14:35,897 --> 00:14:44,238
In one cycle, the sender is busy for W times L over R. That is W packets of 1 ms each.

137
00:14:44,688 --> 00:14:54,885
W equal to 1 is stop-and-wait. The sender is busy for 1 ms of 10. U is 1 over 10, which is 0 point 1.

138
00:14:55,335 --> 00:15:04,274
W equal to 3: the sender is busy for 3 ms of 10. U is 3 over 10, which is 0 point 3.

139
00:15:04,724 --> 00:15:11,423
W equal to 10: the 10 packets fill the whole cycle. U is 10 over 10, which is 1.

140
00:15:11,873 --> 00:15:22,795
W equal to 12 gives 12 over 10. But the sender cannot be busy for more than the whole cycle. Packets 11 and 12 go into the next cycle.

141
00:15:23,245 --> 00:15:29,475
So U is the minimum of 1 and the ratio. For W equal to 12, U is 1.

142
00:15:29,925 --> 00:15:37,775
This plot shows U against W for the example. U grows in a straight line with W, and then stays at 1.

143
00:15:38,225 --> 00:15:49,361
The ideal window size makes U exactly 1. Then the window fills the whole cycle: W times L over R equals RTT plus L over R.

144
00:15:49,811 --> 00:15:59,497
Divide both sides by L over R. Then W upper bound is RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R.

145
00:15:59,947 --> 00:16:07,456
Here, 9 plus 1, divided by 1, is 10 packets. The plot bends at W equal to 10.

146
00:16:07,906 --> 00:16:11,511
A larger window does not help. U stays at 1.

147
00:16:11,961 --> 00:16:21,647
The points W equal to 1 and W equal to 3, from the bars, lie on the line, at 0 point 1 and 0 point 3.

148
00:16:22,097 --> 00:16:30,865
The old slide 50 writes the same formula with t T for RTT plus L over R, and t I for L over R.

149
00:16:31,315 --> 00:16:40,275
Now the question bank. Question 30 asks for a table that compares Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat. Pause the video and try it.

150
00:16:40,725 --> 00:16:52,159
The given row is timers. Go-Back-N has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. Selective Repeat has one timer for each unACKed packet.

151
00:16:52,609 --> 00:16:57,281
Both protocols can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

152
00:16:57,731 --> 00:17:06,883
Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs. ACK n covers all packets up to n. Selective Repeat ACKs each packet individually.

153
00:17:07,333 --> 00:17:18,576
When an out-of-order packet arrives, the Go-Back-N receiver discards it and re-ACKs the highest in-order sequence number. The Selective Repeat receiver buffers it and ACKs it.

154
00:17:19,026 --> 00:17:27,517
When a timer expires, Go-Back-N resends all unACKed packets. Selective Repeat resends only that packet.

155
00:17:27,967 --> 00:17:39,039
With one loss and N equal to 4, Go-Back-N resends 4 packets, and Selective Repeat resends 1. These are the traces from the start of this video.

156
00:17:39,489 --> 00:17:48,790
The Go-Back-N receiver expects one sequence number only. The Selective Repeat receiver has a window of N, and delivers in order.

157
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:56,003
Selective Repeat also has a limit: the window size is at most half of the sequence number space.

158
00:17:56,453 --> 00:18:02,383
Questions 31 to 33 are short calculations. Pause the video and try them.

159
00:18:02,833 --> 00:18:10,620
Question 31 gives a sequence number field of 8 bits. It asks for the size of the sequence number space.

160
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:21,011
A field of 8 bits has 2 to the power 8 values. So the space has 256 numbers, from 0 to 255.

161
00:18:21,461 --> 00:18:29,291
Question 32 works backwards. The space has 2048 numbers. How many bits does the field need?

162
00:18:29,741 --> 00:18:39,149
2 to the power 10 is 1024, so 2 to the power 11 is 2048. So the field needs 11 bits.

163
00:18:39,599 --> 00:18:48,751
Question 33 gives Selective Repeat with a sequence number space of 4096. It asks for the largest window.

164
00:18:49,201 --> 00:19:01,083
The window size must be at most half of the space. 4096 divided by 2 is 2048. So the maximum window is 2048.

165
00:19:01,533 --> 00:19:07,848
Question 34 asks you to derive the ideal window size. Pause the video and try it.

166
00:19:08,298 --> 00:19:17,578
The sender sends the first packet at time 0. Its ACK returns at time RTT plus L over R, as on the last slides.

167
00:19:18,028 --> 00:19:27,799
Until that ACK arrives, the sender can send only the packets of its window. W packets keep it busy for W times L over R.

168
00:19:28,249 --> 00:19:37,081
The sender is never idle if W times L over R is greater than or equal to RTT plus L over R.

169
00:19:37,531 --> 00:19:44,293
So the largest useful window is RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R.

170
00:19:44,743 --> 00:19:56,135
Split the fraction. L over R divided by L over R is 1. So W upper bound is 1, plus RTT divided by L over R.

171
00:19:56,585 --> 00:20:12,308
In the picture, L over R is 1 ms, and RTT is 9 ms. 9 plus 1, divided by 1, is 10. So 10 packets fill the cycle, and the first ACK arrives when packet 10 ends.

172
00:20:12,758 --> 00:20:19,670
Question 35 asks for the utilization with a given window W. Pause the video and try it.

173
00:20:20,120 --> 00:20:30,317
If W is smaller than the upper bound, the sender is busy for W times L over R in each cycle of RTT plus L over R.

174
00:20:30,767 --> 00:20:39,578
So U is the ratio of these two times: W times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

175
00:20:40,028 --> 00:20:46,983
If W is equal to or larger than the upper bound, the sender is never idle, and U is 1.

176
00:20:47,433 --> 00:21:01,043
Both cases together give one expression: U equals the minimum of 1, and W times L over R divided by RTT plus L over R. This is note 2 of slide 49.

177
00:21:01,493 --> 00:21:07,744
Now Problem 3 of Problem Set 3. Node A sends frames to node C through node B.

178
00:21:08,194 --> 00:21:16,962
Link A B is 2000 miles long and runs at 300 kbps. It uses a sliding window of 5.

179
00:21:17,412 --> 00:21:21,913
Link B C is 500 miles long and uses stop-and-wait.

180
00:21:22,363 --> 00:21:25,158
Part a asks for the minimum rate of link B C.

181
00:21:25,608 --> 00:21:40,755
All data frames are 1500 bits. The ACKs have negligible length. The propagation delay is 12 microseconds per mile on both lines. The lines are full duplex, and there are no errors.

182
00:21:41,205 --> 00:21:47,605
Part b swaps the protocols: stop-and-wait on A B, and a window of 5 on B C.

183
00:21:48,055 --> 00:21:53,431
The buffers of B do not flood if frames leave B at least as fast as they arrive.

184
00:21:53,881 --> 00:21:59,683
So the throughput of B C must be at least the throughput of A B. Pause the video and try it.

185
00:22:00,133 --> 00:22:17,819
Part a, link A B first. The transmission time d is L over R: 1500 bits divided by 300 kbps. 300 kbps is 300 times 10 to the power 3 bits per second.

186
00:22:18,269 --> 00:22:27,847
1500 divided by 300 thousand is 0 point 0 0 5 seconds, which is 5 ms.

187
00:22:28,297 --> 00:22:39,817
The propagation delay P is 12 microseconds per mile, times 2000 miles. That is 24 thousand microseconds, or 24 ms.

188
00:22:40,267 --> 00:22:52,918
The first ACK comes back after d plus 2 P. That is 5 plus 2 times 24, which is 53 ms. This is the cycle, RTT plus L over R.

189
00:22:53,368 --> 00:23:02,776
With a window of 5, the sender is busy for 5 times 5, which is 25 ms of each 53 ms cycle.

190
00:23:03,226 --> 00:23:14,255
So the efficiency E is 25 divided by 53, which is 0 point 4 7 1 7. It is less than 1, so E is this ratio.

191
00:23:14,705 --> 00:23:26,695
The throughput is E times the rate. 0 point 4 7 1 7 times 300 kbps is 141 point 5 kbps.

192
00:23:27,145 --> 00:23:35,763
Divide by 1500 bits per frame. That gives 94 point 3 4 frames per second into node B.

193
00:23:36,213 --> 00:23:50,421
Now link B C, with an unknown rate x. Its propagation delay P is 12 microseconds per mile, times 500 miles. That is 6000 microseconds, or 6 ms.

194
00:23:50,871 --> 00:24:04,119
One cycle T is the transmission time, 1500 over x, plus 2 times 6 ms. So T is 1500 over x, plus 12 ms.

195
00:24:04,569 --> 00:24:13,679
Stop-and-wait sends one frame per cycle. So the throughput is 1 over T frames per second. The green curve shows it against x.

196
00:24:14,129 --> 00:24:25,243
A faster link makes 1500 over x small. But the 12 ms of propagation stays. So T can never be less than 12 ms.

197
00:24:25,693 --> 00:24:35,165
Even with an infinite rate, the throughput is 1 over 0 point 0 1 2 seconds. That is 83 point 3 frames per second.

198
00:24:35,615 --> 00:24:48,074
83 point 3 frames per second, times 1500 bits, is 125 thousand bits per second, or 125 kbps.

199
00:24:48,524 --> 00:24:54,583
From part a, node B receives 94 point 3 4 frames per second from A.

200
00:24:55,033 --> 00:25:07,107
83 point 3 is less than 94 point 3 4. So no rate on link B C is enough, and the buffers of B flood. This is the answer of the solution.

201
00:25:07,557 --> 00:25:15,579
Part b. Now link A B uses stop-and-wait. Its cycle is the same 53 ms as in part a.

202
00:25:16,029 --> 00:25:26,162
Stop-and-wait sends one frame per cycle. 1 over 0 point 0 5 3 seconds is 18 point 8 7 frames per second.

203
00:25:26,612 --> 00:25:37,364
Times 1500 bits, that is 28 thousand 302 bits per second, or 28 point 3 kbps into node B.

204
00:25:37,814 --> 00:25:54,241
Link B C now has a window of 5. Its efficiency is the minimum of 1, and 5 times 1500 over x, divided by 1500 over x plus 0 point 0 1 2 seconds.

205
00:25:54,691 --> 00:26:06,893
Multiply the top and the bottom by x. The ratio becomes 7500, divided by 0 point 0 1 2 x plus 1500.

206
00:26:07,343 --> 00:26:27,994
At x equal to 500 kbps, 0 point 0 1 2 times 500 thousand is 6000, and 7500 over 7500 is 1. For lower rates the ratio is larger. So E is 1, and the throughput of B C is x.

207
00:26:28,444 --> 00:26:47,879
The solution first assumes that E is less than 1. It sets the throughput equal to 28 point 3 kbps. Multiply out and solve for x. That gives about 5929 bits per second, or 5 point 9 3 kbps.

208
00:26:48,329 --> 00:27:02,195
Then it checks the assumption. At this x, the ratio is 7500 over 1571, which is 4 point 8. That is more than 1, so the assumption is false.

209
00:27:02,645 --> 00:27:13,995
So E is 1, and the throughput of B C is x. The rate of link B C must be 28 point 3 kbps or more. This matches the solution.

210
00:27:14,445 --> 00:27:24,151
To recap. The Selective Repeat receiver ACKs each packet in its window individually. It buffers out-of-order packets, and delivers in order.

211
00:27:24,601 --> 00:27:30,041
The sender keeps one timer per packet, and resends only the packet that timed out.

212
00:27:30,491 --> 00:27:34,715
ACK n moves send base only when n is equal to send base.

213
00:27:35,165 --> 00:27:41,736
A packet from the previous window gets a new ACK. Any other packet outside the window is ignored.

214
00:27:42,186 --> 00:27:49,589
With one loss and a window of 4, Go-Back-N resends 4 packets, and Selective Repeat resends 1.

215
00:27:50,039 --> 00:27:57,719
A k-bit field gives 2 to the power k sequence numbers. The window size must be at most half of this space.

216
00:27:58,169 --> 00:28:09,539
The utilization is the minimum of 1, and W times L over R divided by RTT plus L over R. The next video is about TCP.
